{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0158G8RjXKtmzgBiaP1wtV7Z","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Draft reference gap analysis\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval needs, none requiring a rewrite of the paper story.\n4\t\n5\t## Overall assessment\n6\tThe draft is densely cited on Bradley-Terry estimation (ties, order effects, existence of the MLE, penalised fits, asymptotics) and on sampling uncertainty. It also cites the single-point influence-function paper, the dropping-data robustness paper, the two Arena integrity papers, and the Arena and MT-Bench data sources. All quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are out of scope here. The gaps concern the paper's actual subject (small adversarial edits to comparison data), the caveats on first-order approximations, and the sports data.\n7\t\n8\t## Targets\n9\t\n10\t### 1. Poisoning and manipulation literature (high, positioning) — Related Work\n11\t- **Anchor:** the \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n12\t- **Why:** the paper asks how few dropped or reversed rows displace rank 1, which is a data-poisoning or manipulation question. No citation covers influence-based poisoning, label-flip attacks, or adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation and rating systems. The \"generic, evaluator-verified probe\" framing cannot be positioned without this, and reviewers would likely flag it.\n13\t- **Search topics:** influence-function-based data poisoning; label-flipping attacks on logistic or pairwise models; adversarial robustness of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons; manipulation-resistant rating systems (Elo / BT).\n14\t\n15\t### 2. Accuracy of first-order group-removal approximations (medium, background) — Problem Setting and Method\n16\t- **Anchor:** \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change…\". The \"Linearisation error\" hypothesis in the analysis section relies on the same claim.\n17\t- **Why:** the claim that the additive first-order heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2% budget is uncited. Only single-point influence and the dropping-data robustness paper are cited.\n18\t- **Search topics:** accuracy of influence functions for group effects; infinitesimal-jackknife approximation error when dropping data in M-estimators or logistic regression.\n19\t\n20\t### 3. Sports rating context (medium, benchmark_context) — Problem Setting and Method\n21\t- **Anchor:** Table 1 row \"Sports: NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first\".\n22\t- **Why:** NBA results are used as a fourth BT design (and called a \"sports proxy\" in Limitations) without any citation to BT or Elo-style rating of sports teams. One or two references would justify the design and the home-team-first and tie conventions. The Arena and MT-Bench rows are already cited.\n23\t- **Search topics:** Bradley-Terry rating of sports teams with home advantage; Elo rating applied to basketball or team sports.\n24\t\n25\t## Deliberately not flagged\n26\t- BT model variants and estimation: already over-supported.\n27\t- Uncertainty of rankings: adequately cited.\n28\t- LLM-judge and Arena data descriptions: covered by the Chiang and Zheng citations.\n29\t- Numerical results, reference-policy scores and validity checks: grounded in `paper_data.json`.\n30\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabddf67-81ef-4927-9bff-05e0f3d2692f","uuid":"dddfe48b-d9f9-4408-918c-b42a5c8d6491","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:42:22.144Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/workspace/paper/draft_reference_gaps.md","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. Three targeted retrieval needs, none requiring a rewrite of the paper story.\n\n## Overall assessment\nThe draft is densely cited on Bradley-Terry estimation (ties, order effects, existence of the MLE, penalised fits, asymptotics) and on sampling uncertainty. It also cites the single-point influence-function paper, the dropping-data robustness paper, the two Arena integrity papers, and the Arena and MT-Bench data sources. All quantitative claims are grounded in `paper_data.json` and are out of scope here. The gaps concern the paper's actual subject (small adversarial edits to comparison data), the caveats on first-order approximations, and the sports data.\n\n## Targets\n\n### 1. Poisoning and manipulation literature (high, positioning) — Related Work\n- **Anchor:** the \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n- **Why:** the paper asks how few dropped or reversed rows displace rank 1, which is a data-poisoning or manipulation question. No citation covers influence-based poisoning, label-flip attacks, or adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation and rating systems. The \"generic, evaluator-verified probe\" framing cannot be positioned without this, and reviewers would likely flag it.\n- **Search topics:** influence-function-based data poisoning; label-flipping attacks on logistic or pairwise models; adversarial robustness of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons; manipulation-resistant rating systems (Elo / BT).\n\n### 2. Accuracy of first-order group-removal approximations (medium, background) — Problem Setting and Method\n- **Anchor:** \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change…\". The \"Linearisation error\" hypothesis in the analysis section relies on the same claim.\n- **Why:** the claim that the additive first-order heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2% budget is uncited. Only single-point influence and the dropping-data robustness paper are cited.\n- **Search topics:** accuracy of influence functions for group effects; infinitesimal-jackknife approximation error when dropping data in M-estimators or logistic regression.\n\n### 3. Sports rating context (medium, benchmark_context) — Problem Setting and Method\n- **Anchor:** Table 1 row \"Sports: NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first\".\n- **Why:** NBA results are used as a fourth BT design (and called a \"sports proxy\" in Limitations) without any citation to BT or Elo-style rating of sports teams. One or two references would justify the design and the home-team-first and tie conventions. The Arena and MT-Bench rows are already cited.\n- **Search topics:** Bradley-Terry rating of sports teams with home advantage; Elo rating applied to basketball or team sports.\n\n## Deliberately not flagged\n- BT model variants and estimation: already over-supported.\n- Uncertainty of rankings: adequately cited.\n- LLM-judge and Arena data descriptions: covered by the Chiang and Zheng citations.\n- Numerical results, reference-policy scores and validity checks: grounded in `paper_data.json`.\n","numLines":30,"startLine":1,"totalLines":30}}}
