{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HzFtbkoecBusrDEwupwdKs","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"status\": \"ready\",\n3\t  \"summary\": \"The Bradley-Terry background is densely cited, but the paper's actual subject (small adversarial or influential edits to ranking data), the accuracy of first-order group-removal approximations, and the sports comparison data are thinly supported and justify three targeted retrievals.\",\n4\t  \"missing_literatures\": [\n5\t    \"data poisoning and adversarial manipulation of ranking / rank-aggregation / rating systems\",\n6\t    \"accuracy and failure modes of first-order (group) influence approximations\",\n7\t    \"Bradley-Terry / Elo rating of sports teams (NBA) as a comparison-data setting\"\n8\t  ],\n9\t  \"rewrite_targets\": [\n10\t    {\n11\t      \"target_id\": \"related-work-poisoning-positioning\",\n12\t      \"section\": \"Related Work\",\n13\t      \"priority\": \"high\",\n14\t      \"citation_intent\": \"positioning\",\n15\t      \"reason\": \"The paper's core question is how few edited or reversed comparison rows change the top rank, i.e. a data-poisoning / manipulation attack on a ranking. Related Work covers BT estimation, sampling uncertainty, influence functions and two Arena-specific manipulation papers, but cites nothing on influence-based poisoning, label-flip attacks, or adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation and rating systems. A reviewer would ask how the probe relates to that literature, and the draft cannot position its 'generic, evaluator-verified probe' without it.\",\n16\t      \"anchor_text\": \"Influence and robustness to dropping data / Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards paragraphs\",\n17\t      \"search_topics\": [\n18\t        \"data poisoning attacks using influence functions\",\n19\t        \"label-flipping attacks on logistic or pairwise-comparison models\",\n20\t        \"adversarial manipulation and robustness of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons\",\n21\t        \"manipulation-resistant rating systems and Elo/Bradley-Terry\"\n22\t      ]\n23\t    },\n24\t    {\n25\t      \"target_id\": \"method-additivity-linearisation-support\",\n26\t      \"section\": \"Problem Setting and Method\",\n27\t      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n28\t      \"citation_intent\": \"background\",\n29\t      \"reason\": \"Property (i) in the influence-search subsection (additive per-row effects, inaccurate at a 2% budget) and the 'Linearisation error' hypothesis in the analysis section assert limits of first-order approximations for multi-row removal without any citation. Only the single-point influence-function paper and the dropping-data robustness paper are cited. Literature on group-influence accuracy and on when infinitesimal-jackknife / influence approximations fail would ground these caveats.\",\n30\t      \"anchor_text\": \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change\",\n31\t      \"search_topics\": [\n32\t        \"accuracy of influence functions for group effects when removing multiple training points\",\n33\t        \"infinitesimal jackknife approximation error for dropping data in M-estimators / logistic regression\"\n34\t      ]\n35\t    },\n36\t    {\n37\t      \"target_id\": \"designs-sports-rating-context\",\n38\t      \"section\": \"Problem Setting and Method\",\n39\t      \"priority\": \"medium\",\n40\t      \"citation_intent\": \"benchmark_context\",\n41\t      \"reason\": \"The Evaluation designs subsection and Table 1 introduce NBA game results as a fourth design and the Limitations section calls it a 'sports proxy', but no work on Bradley-Terry or Elo-style rating of sports teams is cited. One or two citations would justify using game results as BT comparison data and contextualise the home-team-first and tie conventions. The Arena and MT-Bench rows are already cited.\",\n42\t      \"anchor_text\": \"Sports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015