{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DJ2FJuimsLcCE8fPRiLmJb","type":"tool_result","content":"['status', 'central_topic', 'core_methods', 'comparators', 'benchmarks', 'architectural_shifts', 'result_driven_themes', 'queries', 'forbidden_query_patterns', 'failure_reason']\n{'query': 'sensitivity of leaderboard rankings to evaluation data choices statistical uncertainty of model rankings', 'category': 'result_driven_theme', 'rationale': 'Cross-design comparison and noise calibration of rank stability requires literature on ranking uncertainty and leaderboard reliability.'}\n59:We apply it to held-out data from four evaluation designs that differ in who judges and what is judged: crowd-sourced human votes on chatbot responses \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot}, expert human judgments and GPT-4 judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}, and National Basketball Association (NBA) game results.\n122:\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row.\n135:Table~\\ref{tab:designs} lists the designs. Every design is evaluated in both edit modes, so crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge data contribute one case per mode, and sports data three NBA seasons per mode. The sealed final data are disjoint from the data used to choose the search configuration (verified by content hashes in the task protocol), and anonymised competitor indices carry no model or team names. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#provenance]]\n149:Sports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first & 6 (3 seasons $\\times$ 2) & Teams of one season \\\\\n155:The proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the proposer is deterministic, the three seeds are replicate executions of the same computation and carry no information about sampling variability of the data. Search-time scores were obtained on a separate proxy data collection (different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data) and are reported only as separate context in Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}; they are never compared with the final scores.\n221:\\item \\emph{Linearisation error.} A 2\\% edit of a small dataset is a larger perturbation of $A$ and $p$ than a 2\\% edit of a large one, so the additive first-order approximation may degrade differently across designs.\n238:\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source and the same questions.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabddf67-81ef-4927-9bff-05e0f3d2692f","uuid":"008c938e-17ef-4001-9b16-d51298dd355f","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:42:25.332Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"['status', 'central_topic', 'core_methods', 'comparators', 'benchmarks', 'architectural_shifts', 'result_driven_themes', 'queries', 'forbidden_query_patterns', 'failure_reason']\n{'query': 'sensitivity of leaderboard rankings to evaluation data choices statistical uncertainty of model rankings', 'category': 'result_driven_theme', 'rationale': 'Cross-design comparison and noise calibration of rank stability requires literature on ranking uncertainty and leaderboard reliability.'}\n59:We apply it to held-out data from four evaluation designs that differ in who judges and what is judged: crowd-sourced human votes on chatbot responses \\citep{Chiang2024Chatbot}, expert human judgments and GPT-4 judgments on the same MT-Bench comparisons \\citep{Zheng2023Judging}, and National Basketball Association (NBA) game results.\n122:\\emph{(i) Additivity.} The per-row effects are summed without modelling how $p_i$ and $A$ change after many rows are edited, so the heuristic can be inaccurate at a 2\\% budget even when it is accurate for one row.\n135:Table~\\ref{tab:designs} lists the designs. Every design is evaluated in both edit modes, so crowd, expert-human and LLM-judge data contribute one case per mode, and sports data three NBA seasons per mode. The sealed final data are disjoint from the data used to choose the search configuration (verified by content hashes in the task protocol), and anonymised competitor indices carry no model or team names. % @evidence[[source_evidence/task/task_contract.json#provenance]]\n149:Sports & NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first & 6 (3 seasons $\\times$ 2) & Teams of one season \\\\\n155:The proposer is evaluated once on the sealed final data with three evaluation seeds (201, 202, 203). A seed only resets proposer state; it never selects data or workload. Because the proposer is deterministic, the three seeds are replicate executions of the same computation and carry no information about sampling variability of the data. Search-time scores were obtained on a separate proxy data collection (different Arena partition and NBA seasons, and no expert-human or LLM-judge data) and are reported only as separate context in Appendix~\\ref{app:selection}; they are never compared with the final scores.\n221:\\item \\emph{Linearisation error.} A 2\\% edit of a small dataset is a larger perturbation of $A$ and $p$ than a 2\\% edit of a large one, so the additive first-order approximation may degrade differently across designs.\n238:\\paragraph{Design confounds and few cases.} The four designs are specific datasets, not random draws from design classes; they are confounded with size, number of competitors, margin and tie rate. Three of the four designs have one case per edit mode. The NBA data are game results, not preference or judge data, and serve as a sports proxy. Expert-human and LLM-judge data share a single source and the same questions.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
