{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgVN3Pq8bcV52k8Qi3N9y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YMDTCwhM8ty9syzF94u82H","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\nplan = {\n \"status\": \"ready\",\n \"central_topic\": \"Sensitivity of Bradley-Terry pairwise-comparison leaderboard rankings (rank-1 displacement) to small, evaluator-verified drop/flip edits of comparison data, compared across crowd-vote, expert-human, LLM-judge and sports (NBA) designs; post-draft gaps: poisoning/manipulation positioning, accuracy of first-order group-removal approximations, and sports rating context.\",\n \"core_methods\": [\n  \"Ridge-penalised Bradley-Terry maximum-likelihood fitting of pairwise comparisons\",\n  \"First-order influence-function heuristic with additive per-row effects for choosing drop/flip edits\",\n  \"Exact-refit verification of edited comparison data\"\n ],\n \"comparators\": [\n  \"Influence-function-based data poisoning and label-flip attacks on logistic or pairwise models\",\n  \"Adversarial manipulation of rank aggregation and rating systems (Elo/Bradley-Terry)\",\n  \"Group-influence and infinitesimal-jackknife approximations for multi-point removal\"\n ],\n \"benchmarks\": [\n  \"NBA regular-season game results (2008, 2012, 2015) as Bradley-Terry/Elo-style sports comparison data\"\n ],\n \"architectural_shifts\": [],\n \"result_driven_themes\": [\n  \"Additive first-order approximation may degrade at a 2% edit budget and differ across designs (linearisation-error hypothesis)\",\n  \"Minimal-perturbation sensitivity is a data-poisoning/manipulation question on rankings and needs positioning against that literature\"\n ],\n \"queries\": [\n  {\n   \"query\": \"influence function data poisoning attack training set perturbation logistic regression\",\n   \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n   \"rationale\": \"Draft target related-work-poisoning-positioning (high): the Related Work paragraphs on influence and dropping data cite no influence-based poisoning work; needed to position the evaluator-verified drop/flip probe against poisoning attacks that use influence to choose edits.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"query\": \"label flipping poisoning attack adversarial robustness pairwise comparison rank aggregation manipulation\",\n   \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n   \"rationale\": \"Draft target related-work-poisoning-positioning (high): flip mode is a label-flip edit on pairwise data; need adversarial manipulation/robustness of rank aggregation from pairwise comparisons, beyond the two Arena-specific integrity papers already cited.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"query\": \"manipulation-resistant rating systems Elo Bradley-Terry strategic manipulation of match outcomes\",\n   \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n   \"rationale\": \"Draft target related-work-poisoning-positioning (high): the Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards paragraph lacks general literature on manipulating rating systems, needed for the 'generic probe' positioning.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"query\": \"accuracy of influence functions for group effects removing multiple training points infinitesimal jackknife approximation error\",\n   \"category\": \"core_method\",\n   \"rationale\": \"Draft target method-additivity-linearisation-support (medium): supports the uncited claim in property (i) Additivity and the Linearisation error hypothesis that first-order additive approximations can be inaccurate for multi-row removal at a 2% budget; complements the cited single-point influence and dropping-data papers without repeating them.\"\n  },\n  {\n   \"query\": \"Bradley-Terry model home advantage rating of basketball and sports teams Elo\",\n   \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n   \"rationale\": \"Draft target designs-sports-rating-context (medium): justifies using NBA game results (home team listed first, Table 1) as a Bradley-Terry comparison design and contextualises the home-team and tie conventions and the 'sports proxy' caveat.\"\n  }\n ],\n \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n  \"generic optimization or benchmarking phrasing\",\n  \"paper-writing or scientific-method phrasing\",\n  \"chronology or attempt/cycle/search-history phrasing (e.g. cycle, attempt, program_1)\",\n  \"claims that the re-linearised or prefix-line-search variants beat the baseline (no scored evidence)\",\n  \"titles, authors or topics of any paper directly studying dropping preferences to change LLM leaderboard top ranks (excluded by the task's no-access rule)\",\n  \"re-retrieval of Bradley-Terry variants, ties/order effects, MLE existence, asymptotics or ranking-uncertainty literature already in references.bib\",\n  \"re-retrieval of Koh & Liang, Broderick et al., Chiang et al., Zheng et al., Min et al. (rigging) or Singh et al. (Leaderboard Illusion)\",\n  \"unrelated deep-learning architecture or GPU training queries\"\n ],\n \"failure_reason\": \"\"\n}\njson.dump(plan, open('postdraft_reference_query_plan.json','w'), indent=2)\nmd = \"\"\"# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. Five queries, all driven by `draft_reference_gaps.{md,json}`; no retrieval performed in this stage.\n\n## Central topic\nSensitivity of Bradley-Terry leaderboard rankings (rank-1 displacement) to small, evaluator-verified drop/flip edits across crowd, expert-human, LLM-judge and NBA sports designs. This plan only fills the three gaps exposed by the draft.\n\n## Existing bibliography (not re-searched)\nBradley-Terry variants, ties/order effects, MLE existence, asymptotics and ridge fits; ranking uncertainty; Koh & Liang influence functions; Broderick et al. dropping-data robustness; Min et al. (vote rigging); Singh et al. (Leaderboard Illusion); Chiang et al. (Arena); Zheng et al. (MT-Bench).\n\n## Queries\n\n### 1. Poisoning and manipulation positioning (high, Related Work)\nAnchor: \"Influence and robustness to dropping data\" and \"Integrity of crowd-sourced leaderboards\" paragraphs.\n1. `influence function data poisoning attack training set perturbation logistic regression` (baseline_comparator): influence-based poisoning that chooses edits, the closest relative of the drop/flip probe.\n2. `label flipping poisoning attack adversarial robustness pairwise comparison rank aggregation manipulation` (baseline_comparator): flip mode as a label-flip edit; adversarial robustness of rank aggregation.\n3. `manipulation-resistant rating systems Elo Bradley-Terry strategic manipulation of match outcomes` (baseline_comparator): general manipulation of rating systems beyond the two Arena papers.\n\n### 2. First-order group-removal accuracy (medium, Problem Setting and Method)\nAnchor: \"(i) Additivity. The per-row effects are summed without modelling how p_i and A change...\" and the \"Linearisation error\" hypothesis.\n4. `accuracy of influence functions for group effects removing multiple training points infinitesimal jackknife approximation error` (core_method): supports the claim that additive first-order approximations can be inaccurate at a 2% budget. One query covers both group-influence and infinitesimal-jackknife error.\n\n### 3. Sports rating context (medium, Problem Setting and Method)\nAnchor: Table 1 row \"Sports: NBA regular-season games of 2008, 2012, 2015; home team listed first\" and the \"sports proxy\" limitation.\n5. `Bradley-Terry model home advantage rating of basketball and sports teams Elo` (benchmark_context): justifies NBA results as BT data and contextualises the home-first and tie conventions.\n\n## Exclusions\n- No re-retrieval of BT variants, ties, MLE existence, asymptotics or ranking uncertainty (the gap memo marks them over-supported).\n- No re-retrieval of the Arena/MT-Bench data papers or already-cited influence, dropping-data and rigging papers.\n- No queries about papers that directly study dropping preferences to change LLM leaderboard top ranks (excluded by the task's no-access rule).\n- No claims about the re-linearised or prefix-search variants, since the run has no scored winner.\n\"\"\"\nopen('postdraft_reference_query_plan.md','w').write(md)\nEOF\npython3 -c \"import json;print(len(json.load(open('postdraft_reference_query_plan.json'))['queries']))\""},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":315,"cache_read_input_tokens":26787,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":315,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"aabddf67-81ef-4927-9bff-05e0f3d2692f","uuid":"78d66e56-bcc0-4837-b073-28d82ff4f541","timestamp":"2026-10-28T21:42:45.220Z","request_id":"req_011CgVN3PWHr8bwXGPGhEuak"}
